# Line endings: LF in the index and LF in every checkout.
#
# **Why this repository needs the rule more than most.** `tools/fork.mjs --check`
# compares the shipped grammar against what the fork tool produces **byte for byte**,
# and `JSON.stringify` emits LF on every platform. `actions/checkout` on a Windows
# runner checks out with `core.autocrlf=true`, so without this file the working tree
# holds CRLF while the generator writes LF — the comparison fails, and it fails only on
# Windows. Measured on run 45881297051: `Grammar (windows-latest, node 20)` and
# `(node 22)` both reported `fork: syntaxes/tension.tmLanguage.json is stale`, while
# both ubuntu jobs passed the same commit.
#
# `eol=lf` normalises on commit *and* on checkout, so the two platforms agree by
# construction rather than by a workflow step that rewrites files after the fact.
#
# This mirrors the rule the Tension product repository already carries
# (`* text=auto eol=lf` plus explicit `eol=lf` for JSON), so a file copied between the
# two trees has the same bytes on both sides.

* text=auto eol=lf

# The file the gate compares byte for byte. Covered by the blanket rule above; named
# because it is the reason this file exists, and a future reader should not have to
# infer that from `* text=auto`.
*.tmLanguage.json text eol=lf

# Manifests and data: `package.json`, `package-lock.json`, `language-configuration.json`.
# A JSON file is text, and a CRLF one makes every JSON diff look rewritten.
*.json text eol=lf

# The fixtures are guest programs copied from the Tension repository, where they are LF.
# A CRLF copy here would still tokenize (the harness splits on `\r\n`), but it would stop
# being a faithful copy — and the assertions name lines of these files.
*.tension text eol=lf
*.ten text eol=lf

# Markdown, the workflow and the JavaScript/MJS sources: covered by the blanket rule,
# listed so the intent is legible without knowing what `text=auto` decides.
*.md    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf

# Binary: never converted, never diffed. `*.vsix` is gitignored, but a packaged
# extension that ever gets committed must not be mangled by an EOL rule.
*.vsix binary
*.png  binary
